CAT = "iespHeights"; SEG = "HGT"
# Unit 7 of Safe Pass: risk assessment for heights. General Application Regulations 2007 Part 4
# Chapter 1 (Regs 103: top guard-rail at least 950 mm, gap no more than 470 mm; 114 ladders;
# 115 fragile surfaces; 119: construction working platforms with a fall of 2 m or more inspected within
# the previous 7 days); HSA work at height briefing notes; Construction Regulations 2013 Schedule 5 (CSCS).
Q = [
('In Irish law, what counts as work at height?',
 'Work anywhere a fall could cause injury', ['Only work more than 2 metres up', 'Only work done on a scaffold', 'Only work done on roofs, ladders and scaffolds'],
 'The HSA defines work at height as work in any place, including at or below ground level, where a person could be injured if they fell. There is no minimum height.'),
('Which of these is work at height under the HSA definition?',
 'Working at ground level beside a trench', ['Sitting in a site office chair', 'Walking up the office stairs', 'Driving a van along a public road'],
 'Working next to an excavation, on trestles or on a flat roof all count. Using ordinary stairs or sitting in a chair does not.'),
('Which work at height measure should be chosen first?',
 'A guard rail or working platform', ['A full body safety harness', 'An air bag below the work', 'A safety net under the edge'],
 'Collective measures that prevent a fall, such as guard rails and platforms, come before measures that only reduce the distance or consequences of a fall.'),
('How high above the edge must the top guard rail be on construction work at height?',
 'At least 950 mm', ['At least 600 mm', 'At least 750 mm', 'At least 1,500 mm'],
 'Regulation 103 of the General Application Regulations 2007 requires the top guard rail to be at least 950 mm above the edge from which a worker could fall.'),
('What is the largest gap allowed between guard rails on a construction working platform?',
 '470 mm', ['300 mm', '600 mm', '750 mm'],
 'Regulation 103 says any intermediate guard rail must be placed so that no gap between it and other protection exceeds 470 mm.'),
('What do toe boards on a scaffold platform mainly prevent?',
 'Tools and materials falling off the edge', ['Workers slipping on the wet boards', 'Rust forming on the scaffold tubes', 'The wind lifting the scaffold boards out of place'],
 "Toe boards stop objects being kicked or knocked off the platform onto people below, and stop a worker's foot sliding under the rail."),
('A working platform on a construction site has a fall of 2 m or more. It must have been inspected within how long before use?',
 'The previous 7 days', ['The previous 14 days', 'The previous 28 days', 'The previous 3 months'],
 'Regulation 119 says such a platform must not be used unless it has been inspected in that position within the previous 7 days.'),
('Apart from the regular check, when else must a scaffold be inspected?',
 'After bad weather or damage', ['Only when it is painted', 'Only when the client asks', 'Never after it is first built'],
 'Work equipment for work at height must be inspected after exceptional circumstances, such as storms or impact, that could affect its safety.'),
('Who may alter a scaffold on site?',
 'A trained and competent scaffolder', ['Any worker who needs more room', 'The crane driver who lifted it in', 'The site electrician on duty'],
 'Removing ties, boards or rails can make a scaffold collapse or leave gaps. Scaffolding tasks are on the CSCS list and need trained scaffolders.'),
('While carrying blocks along a scaffold lift, a bricklayer sees a gap where the scaffolder has removed a guard rail. What should they do?',
 'Stop and report it', ['Use the platform away from the gap', 'Fit a spare rail themselves', 'Tie a rope across the gap'],
 'Do not use an incomplete scaffold or try to repair it. Report it so a competent scaffolder can complete it.'),
('Under Regulation 114, when is a ladder allowed for work at height?',
 'Low-risk work of short duration', ['Any job, if the ladder is tall enough', 'Only jobs that involve heavy loads', 'Only when scaffolds cost too much'],
 'A ladder may be used only where the risk assessment shows that more suitable equipment is not justified because the risk is low and the work short, or the site cannot be altered.'),
('What must a ladder rest on?',
 'A firm, level, stable surface', ['Bricks stacked to gain extra height', 'A pallet laid on soft ground', 'An upturned barrel or drum'],
 'Regulation 114 requires a stable, firm surface of sufficient strength so that the rungs stay level and the ladder cannot sink or slip.'),
('The foot of a leaning ladder sits 0.75 m out from a wall. Roughly what height should its top rest at?',
 '3 metres', ['1.5 metres', '2.25 metres', '4 metres'],
 'The 1 in 4 rule puts the foot one unit out for every four units up, about 75 degrees: 0.75 m out matches a rest point about 3 m up. Too shallow and the foot can slide; too steep and it can tip back.'),
('A ladder is used to reach a landing. What must it do at the top?',
 'Rise far enough above it for a handhold', ['Stop just below the landing edge', 'Rest its top on the gutter', 'Be tied at the bottom and at no other point'],
 'Regulation 114 requires an access ladder to protrude sufficiently above the landing unless another firm handhold is provided. The HSA guide says at least one metre beyond any landing, and the ladder secured.'),
('How should a worker carry materials up a ladder?',
 'Keep a handhold and haul loads up', ['Carry them up in both hands', 'Hold them under one arm while climbing', 'Throw them up to the landing first'],
 'Regulation 114 requires a secure handhold on a ladder. Heavy or bulky items should be lifted by a hoist or rope instead.'),
('What should be done to stop a leaning ladder slipping?',
 'Tie it or use an anti-slip device', ['Lean it at a shallower angle', 'Put a bag of sand on the top rung', 'Stand it on a tarpaulin'],
 'Regulation 114 requires portable ladders to be prevented from slipping by securing the stiles, anti-slip devices or an equivalent arrangement.'),
('What should be done before using a mobile tower scaffold?',
 'Lock the wheels', ['Remove the outriggers', 'Lean it against the wall', 'Let a worker ride on it'],
 'Wheels must be locked whenever the tower is in use, and nobody may stay on a mobile tower while it is moved.'),
('What is a fragile surface?',
 'A surface that could give way under you', ['A surface with fresh paint on it', 'A surface made wet by rain', 'A surface with a slight slope to it'],
 "Fragile roof sheets, roof lights and rotten boards can collapse under a person's weight. Falls through them cause many deaths."),
('Where is the greatest risk of falling through a roof?',
 'Through roof lights and old sheeting', ['Through the gutters at the eaves', 'Through new steel roof decking', 'Through the lead flashing at a chimney'],
 "Roof lights and aged cement or plastic sheets often look solid but will not take a person's weight. They must be covered or guarded."),
('Before working near a fragile surface, what should be provided?',
 'Platforms, covers or guard rails', ['A harness with no anchor point', 'A warning sign and nothing else', 'A spotter standing on the ground'],
 'Regulation 115 requires suitable platforms, coverings or guard rails where work on or near a fragile surface cannot be avoided.'),
('How should a hole in a floor be protected?',
 'With a fixed, marked cover or rail', ['With a loose sheet of plywood', 'With a traffic cone in the middle', 'With a sheet of plastic taped down'],
 'Covers must be strong, fixed so they cannot be moved accidentally and marked to show what they protect. A loose sheet can be lifted or slide.'),
('What is the purpose of a full body harness and lanyard?',
 'To arrest a fall where nothing safer works', ['To replace guard rails on scaffolds', 'To carry tools up a ladder safely', 'To keep workers warm in winter'],
 'Personal fall arrest is used only when collective measures such as guard rails and platforms are not practicable.'),
('Where should a fall arrest lanyard be attached?',
 'To a suitable anchor point', ['To a scaffold guard rail clip', 'To a handrail on a ladder', "To the worker's own belt"],
 'The anchor must be strong enough to hold a falling person. The user must be connected at all times while exposed to the fall.'),
('A worker on a mobile elevating platform falls and is left hanging in their harness. What must already be in place?',
 'A rescue plan and equipment', ['A second worker in a harness', 'A first aid kit in the van', 'A sign at the site gate'],
 'A person hanging in a harness can suffer serious harm if left suspended. A rescue plan and equipment must be ready before work starts.'),
('Waste from an upper floor must be brought down. What is the safe method?',
 'An enclosed chute or a hoist', ['Throwing it over the edge', 'Dropping it after a shout', 'Rolling it down the stairs'],
 'The Construction Regulations ban throwing or dropping materials or waste from a height where they can cause injury. Use chutes, hoists or lower them in a controlled way.'),
('Why is the ground below roof work fenced off with barriers and signs?',
 'To keep people clear of falling objects', ['To store spare scaffold parts safely', 'To mark out the welfare area', 'To park plant overnight'],
 'Barriers and signs keep people out of the area below overhead work, where tools or materials could fall.'),
('Why should work at height be stopped in high winds?',
 'Workers and materials can be blown off', ['Scaffold inspections become invalid in wind', 'Harnesses stop working in wind', 'Ladders stretch in strong gusts'],
 'Strong winds can unbalance workers and lift sheets and boards. The risk assessment should set when work at height stops in bad weather.'),
('Who should be the person working at height?',
 'Someone trained and competent for it', ['The youngest worker on the site', 'Whoever happens to be free that day', 'Any visitor wearing a hard hat'],
 'The HSA says people working at height must be competent, and all work at height must be properly planned and organised.'),
]
